Variables is a named storage location that holds a value, which can be any data type, such as numbers, strings, objects, etc. 
It can be declared using keywords like var, let, const.
Variables can store only one value at a time, so it can't store multiple values for each variables.

If assign a variable without declaring it (var, let, const), it automatically becomes a Global Variable.

• var
The var variables are declared with in a function {} scope, that can't access outside of function {} scope.
The var variables ignores block {} scope.
It's accessible anywhere within the function where it's declared.
The var variables allows both redeclaration and reassignment of it value.
• let
It declared with in the block {} scope only.
The let variables is only accessible OR declared with in a block {} scope only, that can't access outside of a block {} scope.
The let variables does not allows redeclaration of it value in the block {} scope. 
The let variables allows reassignment of it value, means that we can overwrite the values for let variables.
• const
Constant Variable is always a fixed value. It will never change during program execution.
It declared with in the block {} scope only.
The const variables are declared with in a block {} scope only, that can't access outside of a block {} scope.
The const variables does not allows both redeclaration and reassignment of it value.

[Types]
• Local Variables
Local Variables are declared inside the curly braces {} or a function. 
In JavaScript, variables are accessible within the function or block only where they are declared.
Local Variables with the same name can be used in different functions or blocks. 
Local Variables are deleted after the function is completed.
• Global Variables
Global Variables are declared outside and can be accessible from anywhere including function {} scope and block {} scope within the script.
Global Variables are declared outside any function OR block and can be accessed from anywhere in the script, including inside functions and blocks ({}) scope.

[Variable Data Types]
• String
Strings represent text values.
• Number
The number represents numerical values.
• Boolean
True or false is represented by boolean.
• Object
Key-Value pairs are represented by objects.
• Null
It indicates a purposefully empty value.
• Undefined
A declared variable without a value given to it.
